        "asserts": "Fix a constant-step $k$-step method from [[def-linear-multistep-method-consistency-order-and-root-condition]] on $[0,T]$, with $N\\ge k$ and $h=T/N$. For exact solutions with two continuous derivatives, choose a compact state set $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with the exact trajectory in its interior and suppose $f$ is Lipschitz in the state on $[0,T]\\times K$. Convergence means that for every IVP satisfying these conditions and every sequence of starting values whose errors at $t_0,\\ldots,t_{k-1}$ tend to zero as $h\\to0$, each selected run is defined through $t_N$ and its maximum grid error tends to zero. For the sufficiency assertion and order estimates of an implicit method, assume a uniformly local selected branch near the exact trajectory: there are $\\eta,\\varepsilon>0$, independent of $N$ and the step index, such that for all sufficiently small $h$ and each $n=0,\\ldots,N-k$, whenever the preceding history satisfies $\\max_{0\\le j<k}\\|Y_{n+j}-y(t_{n+j})\\|_\\infty\\le\\eta$, the step equation has exactly one root in $\\overline B(y(t_{n+k}),\\varepsilon)\\subset K$, and the method selects that root. Explicit methods need no branch hypothesis. The method converges for every such IVP if and only if it is consistent and satisfies the root condition. More precisely, if its order is at least $p\\ge1$, the exact solution has $p+1$ continuous derivatives, and the starting errors are $O(h^p)$, then its global error is $O(h^p)$.\n\nThe necessity tests $y'=0$ and $y'=1$ have a unique implicit root in all of $\\mathbb R^d$ at every step because $\\alpha_k\\ne0$ and their right-hand sides are independent of the new state. Those runs therefore remain defined even when an unstable mode leaves the sufficiency tube. The sufficiency estimate establishes existence along the selected local branch by keeping every computed history in its neighborhood; it does not assert an implicit root for arbitrary IVPs or step sizes."
