    "prop-rosenbrock-euler-is-one-newton-correction-of-backward-euler": {
      "kind": "proposition",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-backward-euler-step-equation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ define a first-order initial-value problem on an open time-state domain $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$. Given a time $t_n$, step $h_n>0$, and state $y_n$ with $(t_n,y_n)\\in D$, an admissible endpoint $y_{n+1}$ must satisfy $(t_n+h_n,y_{n+1})\\in D$. The backward Euler endpoint is any such $y_{n+1}$ satisfying\n\n$$y_{n+1}=y_n+h_n f(t_n+h_n,y_{n+1}).$$\n\nIts update residual at an approximate endpoint $z$ with $(t_n+h_n,z)\\in D$ is\n\n$$G_n^{\\rm BE}(z)=z-y_n-h_n f(t_n+h_n,z).$$\n\nWhen $f$ is defined at the endpoint, the derivative residual is $G_n^{\\rm BE}(z)/h_n$. The equation can have no, one, or several roots on a restricted admissible branch set; this definition by itself asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is\n**continuously differentiable**, or of class $C^1$, when it is totally\ndifferentiable at every point of $U$ and the entries of its derivative matrix\nare continuous functions on $U$.\n\nFor an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $a\\in U$, a **local inverse of $f$ at\n$a$** is a function $g:W\\to V$ for open neighbourhoods $a\\in V\\subseteq U$\nand $f(a)\\in W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ such that $f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective and\n$g=(f|_V)^{-1}$. If both $f|_V$ and $g$ are $C^1$, this restriction is a\n**local diffeomorphism** at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If every partial derivative $\\partial_jf_i(a)$ of $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ exists, the **Jacobian matrix** is $Jf(a)=(\\partial_jf_i(a))_{i<n,j<m}$. For scalar-valued $f$, its **gradient** is\n\n$$\\nabla f(a):=(\\partial_0f(a),\\ldots,\\partial_{m-1}f(a))\\in\\mathbb R^m,$$\n\nwith coordinates understood in the standard basis ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]). The partial derivatives are those of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].",
          "uses": [
