    "lem-rosenbrock-euler-local-defect-and-residual-error": {
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rosenbrock-euler-step-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be open, let $d\\ge1$, and let $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a vector field ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). For $(t,y)\\in D$, a step size $h>0$, a parameter $\\gamma>0$, and a matrix $\\widetilde J\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$, put\n$$A_{\\gamma,h}(\\widetilde J):=I_d-\\gamma h\\widetilde J.$$\nWhen this matrix is invertible, the **one-stage Rosenbrock step** uses the stage slope $k$ and update\n$$A_{\\gamma,h}(\\widetilde J)k=f(t,y),\\qquad \\Phi_{\\gamma,h}^{\\widetilde J}(t,y):=y+hk.$$\nThe **exact-Jacobian branch** sets $\\widetilde J=D_yf(t,y)$, where $D_yf$ is the Jacobian in the state variables as in [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]. An approximate-Jacobian branch may supply a different $\\widetilde J$; the equation requires the chosen shifted matrix to be invertible. The name **Rosenbrock–Euler** here refers to the case $\\gamma=1$. This definition specifies one linearized step and does not say that it solves the full backward-Euler nonlinear equation.",
