    "thm-rosenbrock-euler-scalar-a-and-l-stability": {
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rosenbrock-euler-step-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be open, let $d\\ge1$, and let $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a vector field ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). For $(t,y)\\in D$, a step size $h>0$, a parameter $\\gamma>0$, and a matrix $\\widetilde J\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$, put\n$$A_{\\gamma,h}(\\widetilde J):=I_d-\\gamma h\\widetilde J.$$\nWhen this matrix is invertible, the **one-stage Rosenbrock step** uses the stage slope $k$ and update\n$$A_{\\gamma,h}(\\widetilde J)k=f(t,y),\\qquad \\Phi_{\\gamma,h}^{\\widetilde J}(t,y):=y+hk.$$\nThe **exact-Jacobian branch** sets $\\widetilde J=D_yf(t,y)$, where $D_yf$ is the Jacobian in the state variables as in [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]. An approximate-Jacobian branch may supply a different $\\widetilde J$; the equation requires the chosen shifted matrix to be invertible. The name **Rosenbrock–Euler** here refers to the case $\\gamma=1$. This definition specifies one linearized step and does not say that it solves the full backward-Euler nonlinear equation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-scalar-rk-a-and-l-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a Runge–Kutta tableau $(A,b,c)$, apply its stage equations to the scalar test equation $y'=\\lambda y$, with $z=h\\lambda$. Whenever $I-zA$ is invertible, the stages satisfy $Y=(I-zA)^{-1}\\mathbf 1\\,y$, and the **scalar stability function** is\n\n$$R(z)=1+z\\,b^\\mathsf T(I-zA)^{-1}\\mathbf 1.$$\n\nThe **scalar stability region** is the set of $z\\in\\mathbb C$ for which the scalar update has $|R(z)|\\le1$. The method is **A-stable** when its rational stability function has no pole in $\\{\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0\\}$ and $|R(z)|\\le1$ throughout that closed half-plane. It is **L-stable** when it is A-stable and $R(z)\\to0$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$ with $\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0$.\n\nThese are properties of the scalar linear test equation and the tableau's rational function. They do not assert nonlinear contractivity or control of nonnormal matrix growth.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ has unique coordinates $z=a+bi$\n([[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]). Define\n$$\\operatorname{Re}z=a,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z=b,\\qquad \\overline z=a-bi,$$\nand define its **modulus** by\n$$|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.$$\nThe real numbers are least-upper-bound complete\n([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), so the nonnegative square root exists and\nis unique by [[thm-of-square-roots]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Complex conjugation is a real-field automorphism satisfying\n$$\\overline{z+w}=\\overline z+\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{\\overline z}=z.$$\nFor every $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$,\n$$z\\overline z=|z|^2,\\qquad |z|\\ge0,\\qquad |z|=0\\Longleftrightarrow z=0,$$\n$$|zw|=|z||w|,\\qquad |z+w|\\le |z|+|w|.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1-1",
          "claim": "For $z\\ne1/\\gamma$, the exact complexified equation in the Statement gives $hk=zy/(1-\\gamma z)$, so the amplification factor is the stated $R_\\gamma(z)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2-1",
          "claim": "Write $z=x+iy$ with $x\\le0$. The denominator $1-\\gamma z$ has real part $1-\\gamma x\\ge1$, so it has no zero there. Using [F4] and [F5], direct expansion gives $|1-\\gamma z|^2-|1+(1-\\gamma)z|^2=-2x+(2\\gamma-1)|z|^2$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3-1",
          "claim": "If $\\gamma\\ge1/2$, both terms on the right of step 2.1 are nonnegative; hence $|R_\\gamma(z)|\\le1$ for all $x\\le0$, and [F3] gives A-stability. If $0<\\gamma<1/2$, choose real $z=-r$ with $r>2/(1-2\\gamma)$. Then the right side is $2r-(1-2\\gamma)r^2<0$, so $|R_\\gamma(-r)|>1$ and A-stability fails.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4-1",
          "claim": "Along $z=-r$ as $r\\to\\infty$, $|R_\\gamma(-r)|\\to|1-\\gamma|/\\gamma$. This is zero exactly when $\\gamma=1$. For $\\gamma=1$, $R_1(z)=1/(1-z)$ and $|R_1(z)|\\le1$ on the closed left half-plane by step 3.1. Also $|z|=|1-(1-z)|\\le1+|1-z|$ by [F5], so for $|z|>1$ we have $|R_1(z)|\\le1/(|z|-1)\\to0$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$ there. Thus [F3] gives the claimed L-stability characterization and the backward-Euler equality.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [
        {
          "case": "A-stability endpoint",
          "input": "gamma=1/2, z=-1",
          "calculation": "R=1+(-1)/(1+1/2)=1/3",
          "result": "the endpoint parameter is included in the A-stable range.",
          "check": "numerical-rosenbrock-fixture",
          "fixture": "one-stage-a-endpoint",
          "asserts": "For scalar stability, consider the exact-arithmetic complexification of the exact-Jacobian one-stage equation. For $\\lambda,y,k\\in\\mathbb C$, $h>0$, $\\gamma>0$, and $z=h\\lambda$, use $(1-\\gamma z)k=\\lambda y$ and $y_+=y+hk$. Equivalently, identify $\\mathbb C$ with $\\mathbb R^2$ and multiplication by $\\lambda$ with the real matrix $C_\\lambda=\\begin{pmatrix}\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&-\\operatorname{Im}\\lambda\\\\\\operatorname{Im}\\lambda&\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\end{pmatrix}$; the corresponding real exact Jacobian is $C_\\lambda$, and the equations become $(I_2-\\gamma h C_\\lambda)k=C_\\lambda y$ and $y_+=y+hk$. This is a scalar stability analysis of exact stage equations; it is not an execution of the finite-precision real algorithm [[alg-rosenbrock-euler-step]]. Use the scalar rational-function criteria in [[def-scalar-rk-a-and-l-stability]] for its amplification function. Its scalar stability function is\n$$R_\\gamma(z)=1+\\frac{z}{1-\\gamma z}=\\frac{1+(1-\\gamma)z}{1-\\gamma z}.$$\nIt has no pole in $\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0$ and satisfies $|R_\\gamma(z)|\\le1$ throughout that half-plane if and only if $\\gamma\\ge\\tfrac12$. Among these parameters, it is L-stable if and only if $\\gamma=1$. In particular, the named Rosenbrock–Euler case $\\gamma=1$ has the backward-Euler factor $R(z)=(1-z)^{-1}$."
        },
        {
