        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-scalar-rk-a-and-l-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a Runge–Kutta tableau $(A,b,c)$, apply its stage equations to the scalar test equation $y'=\\lambda y$, with $z=h\\lambda$. Whenever $I-zA$ is invertible, the stages satisfy $Y=(I-zA)^{-1}\\mathbf 1\\,y$, and the **scalar stability function** is\n\n$$R(z)=1+z\\,b^\\mathsf T(I-zA)^{-1}\\mathbf 1.$$\n\nThe **scalar stability region** is the set of $z\\in\\mathbb C$ for which the scalar update has $|R(z)|\\le1$. The method is **A-stable** when its rational stability function has no pole in $\\{\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0\\}$ and $|R(z)|\\le1$ throughout that closed half-plane. It is **L-stable** when it is A-stable and $R(z)\\to0$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$ with $\\operatorname{Re}z\\le0$.\n\nThese are properties of the scalar linear test equation and the tableau's rational function. They do not assert nonlinear contractivity or control of nonnormal matrix growth.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ has unique coordinates $z=a+bi$\n([[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]). Define\n$$\\operatorname{Re}z=a,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z=b,\\qquad \\overline z=a-bi,$$\nand define its **modulus** by\n$$|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.$$\nThe real numbers are least-upper-bound complete\n([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), so the nonnegative square root exists and\nis unique by [[thm-of-square-roots]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Complex conjugation is a real-field automorphism satisfying\n$$\\overline{z+w}=\\overline z+\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w,\\qquad \\overline{\\overline z}=z.$$\nFor every $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$,\n$$z\\overline z=|z|^2,\\qquad |z|\\ge0,\\qquad |z|=0\\Longleftrightarrow z=0,$$\n$$|zw|=|z||w|,\\qquad |z+w|\\le |z|+|w|.$$",
